/**
@page parsing Parsing and navigation

Navigating from one header to the next with the @c next_ functions is the
primary way to parse a packet. Each @c next_ call validates as it advances,
which is also how a header with more than one possible successor is handled. A
separate family, @c bnt_ctx__find_, reaches a deep header directly when the
layers above it are irrelevant.

@section parsing_navigate Navigating header by header

Parse the outermost header, then descend with a @c next_ call per layer:

@code{.c}
SEC("xdp")
int drop_telnet(struct xdp_md *ctx)
{
    bnt_eth  eth;
    bnt_ipv4 ip;
    bnt_tcp  tcp;
    __u16 dport;
    bool  syn;

    if (bnt_eth__parse(ctx, 0, &eth))  return XDP_PASS;
    if (bnt_eth__next_ipv4(&eth, &ip)) return XDP_PASS;   /* not IPv4 */
    if (bnt_ipv4__next_tcp(&ip, &tcp)) return XDP_PASS;   /* not TCP  */

    if (bnt_tcp__get_dest(&tcp, &dport) || bnt_tcp__is_syn(&tcp, &syn))
        return XDP_PASS;

    return (dport == 23 && syn) ? XDP_DROP : XDP_PASS;
}
@endcode

Each @c next_ call reads the current header, checks that the following protocol
is the one requested, and initializes the destination view. It returns
@c BNT_ERR_BAD_PROTO when the protocol does not match. A view obtained from a
@c next_ call therefore refers to a header that is present and of the expected
type.

@section parsing_branch Handling more than one protocol

A @c next_ call reports a protocol mismatch as @c BNT_ERR_BAD_PROTO, so it
doubles as a protocol test. To accept either of two protocols, attempt each in
turn:

@code{.c}
bnt_tcp tcp;
bnt_udp udp;

if (bnt_ipv4__next_tcp(&ip, &tcp) == BNT_OK) {
    /* ... TCP ... */
} else if (bnt_ipv4__next_udp(&ip, &udp) == BNT_OK) {
    /* ... UDP ... */
}
@endcode

When several protocols are possible, reading the protocol field once and
dispatching is clearer than a chain of attempts:

@code{.c}
__u8 proto;
if (bnt_ipv4__get_protocol(&ip, &proto)) return XDP_PASS;

switch (proto) {
case IPPROTO_TCP: {
    bnt_tcp tcp;
    if (bnt_ipv4__next_tcp(&ip, &tcp)) return XDP_PASS;
    /* ... TCP ... */
    break;
}
case IPPROTO_UDP: {
    bnt_udp udp;
    if (bnt_ipv4__next_udp(&ip, &udp)) return XDP_PASS;
    /* ... UDP ... */
    break;
}
}
@endcode

The protocol field is read with @c bnt_ipv4__get_protocol(),
@c bnt_ipv6__get_l4_proto(), or @c bnt_eth__get_outer_proto().

@c bnt_eth__parse_l3() and @c bnt_ipv4__parse_l4() perform this dispatch in a
single call, initializing whichever of the supplied views matches; see the
reference.

@section parsing_find Finding a header directly

When the layers above the target are irrelevant, @c bnt_ctx__find_ walks the
supported stacks from Ethernet offset 0 and initializes the first header of the
requested type. The same drop, without the intermediate layers:

@code{.c}
SEC("xdp")
int drop_telnet(struct xdp_md *ctx)
{
    bnt_tcp tcp;
    __u16 dport;
    bool  syn;

    if (bnt_ctx__find_tcp(ctx, &tcp)) return XDP_PASS;

    if (bnt_tcp__get_dest(&tcp, &dport) || bnt_tcp__is_syn(&tcp, &syn))
        return XDP_PASS;

    return (dport == 23 && syn) ? XDP_DROP : XDP_PASS;
}
@endcode

A find traverses only the packet stacks BONet supports. The exact stacks each
one covers are listed with the function in the reference. Off a supported path
it reports the boundary through its return value:

- @c BNT_ERR_BAD_PROTO when a supported protocol is present at that layer but is
  not the requested one.
- @c BNT_ERR_UNSUPPORTED for a valid stack the walker does not cover.
- @c BNT_ERR_TOO_DEEP when a depth bound is exhausted. The bounds, and how to
  change them, are in @ref limits.

@section parsing_fragments Fragments

Reaching an L4 header requires that header to be present in the packet. On a
non-first IP fragment it is not, so navigating or finding an L4 header returns
@c BNT_ERR_FRAGMENT. Check for this code wherever the path can carry fragments.

@section parsing_beyond Parsing beyond BONet's headers

To continue past a header BONet does not model, take the offset of the next
header with @c bnt_*__next_off and read from there yourself:

@code{.c}
__u32 off;
struct myhdr hdr;

if (bnt_ipv4__next_off(&ip, &off))             /* offset after the IPv4 header */
    return XDP_PASS;
if (bpf_xdp_load_bytes(ctx, off, &hdr, sizeof(hdr)))
    return XDP_PASS;
/* ... read hdr ... */
@endcode

The offset can also be used directly in inline @c data @c + @c off pointer
code, not only through a load helper.
*/
